  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Dependencies**\n\nI\'m currently delving into the project\'s dependency structure. I\'ve noted the presence of compose BOM and material3, along with Room dependencies. Hold on, the Room runtime is included, and I\'ve spotted `androidx-room-runtime` in the build file, but a navigation-compose dependency is commented out!\n\n\n**Evaluating Architecture Choices**\n\nI\'m now weighing architectural options. The single-screen layout possibility is intriguing, potentially simplifying navigation with Compose state. However, I must evaluate the benefits of a more robust navigation system, specifically `androidx.navigation.compose`. Currently, I\'m analyzing the already active libraries, like `libs.androidx.compose.material.icons.core` to determine a more comprehensive approach.\n\n\n**Expanding Iconography Options**\n\nI\'ve decided to uncomment `libs.androidx.compose.material.icons.extended` to unlock comprehensive material icons, useful for financial categories. Also, I\'m currently torn between state-based navigation and `navigation-compose`, since `libs.androidx.navigation.compose` is also commented. However, after careful consideration for the user\'s needs, I\'m leaning toward state-based navigation for its simplicity and robustness, particularly given the multiple screens and sub-views required. I\'ve also started looking at biometric support since it was requested!\n\n\n**Investigating Biometric Integration**\n\nI\'m now deep-diving into the `androidx.biometric` library. I\'ve confirmed its absence in `libs.versions.toml`, so I\'m considering adding it there or directly in `build.gradle.kts`. After some exploration, I\'m leaning toward the standard `BiometricPrompt` implementation. A critical point is that `BiometricPrompt` requires a `FragmentActivity`, not a `ComponentActivity`. I need to account for this architectural constraint during implementation.\n\n\n**Confirming Activity Inheritance**\n\nI\'ve confirmed that inheriting `MainActivity` from `androidx.fragment.app.FragmentActivity` is the correct approach for `BiometricPrompt` compatibility and have verified that it is available for use. I\'m focusing on why we would inherit from `androidx.fragment.app.FragmentActivity` rather than a more generic `ComponentActivity` for biometrics. Now, I\'m checking existing libraries to see if we can use a custom biometric manager, or have these required libraries. Then I will double check the `libs.versions.toml` to see if `appcompat` or `fragment` are available.\n\n\n**Defining App Structure**\n\nI\'ve confirmed that `androidx.activity.ComponentActivity` is the foundational class. I\'ve also verified `androidx.biometric:biometric` does pull in `androidx.fragment:fragment` and `androidx.activity`, so the inheritance choice for `MainActivity` seems valid. I\'ve added `androidx-biometric` to `libs.versions.toml`, specifically version `1.1.0`. Now, I\'m shifting focus to the app\'s structure and database design. I\'m focusing on defining entities like `UserEntity` and `CategoryEntity` within the Room database, outlining fields such as user ID, email, password, biometric status, category ID, and names.\n\n\n**Refining Data Structures**\n\nI\'m now refining the data structures to align with the user\'s requirements for budget comparison and secure login. I\'m adding `BudgetEntity` with fields for `id`, `userId`, `year`, `month`, and `amount` to track budgets. This allows users to set and compare their monthly budget against savings or expenses. I\'m also considering the implications for biometric login and secure password management. My next step will be to model `Savings` as income minus expenses.\n\n\n**Designing Visual Comparisons**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the visual representation of financial data. My plan involves creating custom Compose-based charts, directly using `Canvas`. I\'ll show income, expenses, savings, and budget comparisons. This will include interactive line/bar charts for historical data, and pie/progress charts for current distribution, as shown in the images.\n\n\n**Analyzing Visual Elements**\n\nI\'m now carefully examining the UI screenshots to define the visual styling. I\'ve noted the color palette, including the deep space blue theme and accent colors. I\'m also capturing details on rounded corners, button styles, the login header with its subtitle, and specific UI elements like the currency buttons and filter rows. I\'ll define these for consistent implementation.\n\n\n**Defining App Sections**\n\nI\'m now outlining the multiple sections of the application. I have identified the following screens: login, register, password reset, and the main dashboard. The dashboard screen is multifaceted, with balance cards, period filtering, transaction and category forms, plus custom charts for financial visualization. I must also add the \"Transacciones\", \"Categor\303\255as\", and \"Conectividad\" tabs.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "I will view the existing theme implementation (`Theme.kt`, `Color.kt`) to ensure my styling choices blend seamlessly with the pre-existing system, or so I can adapt them to the dark cosmic look shown in the user\'s images."
