  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Investigating Error Cause**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the specific code section causing the currency selection request. My current focus is on pinpointing the exact location where the application prompts the user for their primary currency. The goal is to identify why a selection button is missing, preventing the chart from loading. I\'m moving toward reviewing the code for a specific button or list, that would be for the user\'s main currency.\n\n\n**Pinpointing UI Element**\n\nI\'ve shifted my attention to the UI element responsible for currency selection. I\'ve located the string \"Selecciona la moneda principal...\" within `MainActivity.kt`, and this seems key. My hypothesis is this label is related to the missing button the user reported. I\'m now examining the button\'s implementation, looking for potential display issues, and I\'m looking at the Row in the code block.\n\n\n**Analyzing Currency Selection Code**\n\nI\'m now examining the code related to currency selection buttons, specifically within the composable. I\'ve found two `Box` elements, each representing a currency option - Soles and Dollars. These appear to use `clickable` modifiers linked to a `viewModel`. My primary focus is understanding how these elements are rendered, and why one might be missing in a particular context. I\'m now looking at how the `activeColor` and `inactiveColor` variables are determined.\n\n\n**Improving Button Visibility**\n\nI\'ve realized the user\'s issue might stem from the currency selection button\'s visibility or clarity. I\'m now exploring solutions to make this selection much more obvious. I\'m leaning toward implementing a prominent, user-friendly set of buttons within the Analysis, Charts, Settings, or even the empty state of the charts. Alternatively, I\'m considering enhancing the existing banner currency selector, transforming it into more interactive buttons for improved visibility and ease of use.\n\n\n**Refining User Interface**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the user\'s reported problem: a missing button for primary currency selection. I\'m exploring solutions within the UI to make the selection clearer. My thought is, since the UI currently says \"Selecciona la moneda principal...\", the user needs a button labeled \"Seleccionar Moneda Principal\". I\'m considering turning the banner selection into interactive buttons, or adding similar options to the chart card.\n\n\n**Considering UI Alternatives**\n\nI\'m now thinking about other possible causes for the missing currency selection. Perhaps the \"Selecciona la moneda principal...\" text isn\'t associated with the segment selector, or the buttons are too small or not obvious to interact with. To make this clear, I\'ll enhance the banner selector with explicit buttons, including icons and tooltips or messages. Better yet, I could add a new \"Moneda Principal de An\303\241lisis\" selection row or card, with standard button components, or anywhere, with clear Button components like `Button` / `OutlinedButton`.\nI\'ve checked, the missing currency buttons are the only option. Also, the user wants the app to be shorter vertically.\n\n\n**Reorganizing Screen Content**\n\nI\'m now diving into the `DashboardScreen`\'s structure to address the vertical length. I\'ve broken down the existing layout: header, banner, filter card, stat cards, charts, and lower panes. To create a better experience, I\'m considering grouping these elements into collapsible sections. I\'m exploring Material 3\'s collapsible headers or a dedicated \"Secciones\" tab, or perhaps top tabs like \"Resumen & Gr\303\241ficos\" versus \"Operaciones (A\303\261adir)\" versus \"Historial / Detalles\" for a cleaner organization.\n\n\n**Defining Section Navigation**\n\nI\'m now solidifying the section breakdown for the app to address the vertical length issue. It\'s becoming clear that implementing top tabs or collapsible sections perfectly aligns with the user\'s intent to simplify the app. I\'m focusing on these sections: \"Resumen & Gr\303\241ficos\" for the main metrics and charts, \"Operaciones\" for transaction and category management, and \"Historial & Listas\" for detailed transaction lists, categories, and mobile connectivity information. This structure seems clean and provides excellent organization. I am now proceeding with active implementation.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 10
      nanos: 77067077
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
