  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Scroll Functionality**\n\nI\'m currently focused on the `DashboardScreen`\'s scrollable setup. The current implementation utilizes a global `Column` combined with `verticalScroll`. My thinking is that this approach warrants a closer inspection, I want to see if this is the most efficient or performant way. I wonder if there might be a more granular, possibly more performant alternative.\n\n\n**Organizing Dashboard Sections**\n\nI\'m now shifting gears to the main dashboard sections. I\'m restructuring it into three toggle-able sections: \"An\303\241lisis y Gr\303\241ficos,\" \"Registrar Movimiento,\" and \"Detalle y Historial\". I am evaluating UI elements, considering a Material 3 Tab Row, Segmented Button, or custom pill row for smooth section navigation, just below the header. The code is taking form, starting with a state variable `dashboardSection`. I am currently focusing on a wide pill button row for visual appeal.\n\n\n**Refining UI Section Buttons**\n\nI\'ve been working on refining the appearance and functionality of the dashboard section buttons. I\'ve opted for a row of custom pill-shaped buttons. I am using a `Row` layout and setting the `horizontalArrangement` to space them out. Each button is a `Box` that dynamically changes its background based on whether it is selected. I am focusing on visual appeal, usability, and smooth section transitions.\n\n\n**Validating Icon Usage**\n\nI\'m now deep-diving into the icon implementations for my UI section buttons. I\'ve successfully implemented dynamic icon tinting based on the selection state. I\'m focusing on ensuring all icons compile and are the correct ones. So far, `Icons.Default.Analytics` is good. But I\'m now checking `Icons.Default.AddCard` and `Icons.Default.ListAlt`! If they aren\'t standard, I\'ll switch to the more common `Icons.Default.AddCircle`.\n\n\n"
